  "text": "None of the three solutions can be confirmed, and I don't think any of them gives the correct sum.\n\n**Solution 2** is not a derivation. Its own analysis yields 37/30, then it switches to citing an IMC 2021 answer of 2 without support. Its values also don't match its stated classification, so I reject it.\n\n**Solution 3** rests on a shaky premise: it treats the threshold as depending only on whether the graphs contain a triangle, and it asserts that only 2/3 and 5/7 occur. It ignores the δ_χ = 1/2 case from Allen–Böttcher–Griffiths–Kohayakawa–Morris, and it gives no argument that the list is exhaustive, so 29/21 is unsupported.\n\n**Solution 1** uses the right ABGKM values for 3-chromatic graphs, δ_χ ∈ {0, 1/3, 1/2}, and its pentagon/K₆ upper-bound idea is plausible. However, the key steps are asserted rather than proved: the formula (2−d)/(3−2d) for the mixed construction, the claim that the boost inside one part is capped by δ_χ, and especially the claim that exactly these four values occur and no others. The sum 1231/420 depends entirely on those unverified steps. I cannot check the source result here, so I cannot confirm that 1231/420 is the intended answer.\n\nSince none of the answers is verifiable and the most detailed one still depends on unproven claims, I judge that none of the three is reliably correct.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
